## Item text
Hi, I'm Joel with Ruck Authority,
and this is the GORUCK GR1. One bag meant to ruck, travel,
and carry every day. Quick disclosure: GORUCK sent me this bag. And the links below are affiliate links. Here's which ones it does well and where a dedicated ruck
does it better. Now this is the 26 liter
in Ranger Green. I've had it for about two weeks. There's one big main compartment and
webbing on just about every surface. It's a tall bag, about 20 inches, and it feels it. I'm 6'2"
so it works for me and it's a lot of room. If you're shorter there's a 21 liter version that's
about two inches shorter. For this bag my big outing was a lake trail up north with my dog
Jase. In the bag I had a 20 pound plate, my drone in its case, a water bottle, and a first aid kit. I
kept all of that in the main compartment. On the outside I kept Jase's leash and his bowl.
I also brought bear spray. There are a couple smaller pockets on the top which you can
put some pretty small items in. And then you've got this front storage pouch. But beyond that, it's
basically just one big room. That's great when your load is just a plate
and a hard case. Plenty of MOLLE webbing on these. You got it on the front, the sides, and then along the back on the
inside. It's got a whole ecosystem of different attachments, storage options, gear, pretty much
anything you can think of. So that makes up for any lack of dedicated water bottle holders. The GR1
does appear to have a laptop sleeve on the back here. And then if you open this little Velcro
part here, that's where you'll see the back plate that provides the support. So if you've got a
full bag, you could easily have it stand up. This doesn't have the handles on all sides like the
Rucker 5.0, but it does have the standard handle at the top here. The zippers are large, quiet, and
reliable. Definitely have no problem grabbing them. There's no chest strap and there's no hip belt in
the box. That's on purpose. GORUCK built this to ride high on your back and stay stable without a
waist belt. Also, you can add both later if you decide to buy those separately. Ultimately, I didn't
miss the chest strap. Without one, the straps pulled my shoulders back a little bit. My posture is
a work in progress, so it actually felt like it was helping. Plenty of experienced Ruckers will tell
you that a sternum strap matters when the weight goes up. I'm not arguing with them. That's just how
it felt on me with 20 pounds. I did find myself missing the lumbar support a bit. The Rucker 5.0
has a massive lumbar support, so going from that to this felt like a bit of a downgrade. Out of the
box, the straps might feel a bit stiff, but they quickly break in after some use. The plate goes in
this elastic sleeve against your back. It works fine. It's just not quite as refined as the
Rucker 5.0. This is an open sleeve with a couple of straps. Here's the 20 pound plate in the much
looser pocket. The sleeve takes any of GORUCK's plates up to the 45 pound, and they recommend the
30 for this bag because it's thicker and it fills out the sleeve. Whereas the Rucker 5.0 has two
plate sleeves and takes up to 75 pounds, and it holds the plate still for workouts. GORUCK calls
the GR1 highly water resistant. Now, that's not quite the same as waterproof. You'll see
the price on screen. It's not cheap. For that, you'll get a bag that GORUCK will repair or replace under its lifetime
guarantee. So here's where I land.
For pure rucking, the Rucker 5.0 takes the win. It's built for exactly that. If I'm carrying a lot of
gear, the GR2's extra compartment wins. The GR1 is everything in between. It's smaller and
it's lighter than the GR2, and it works as an everyday bag. In my opinion, this is the best looking
bag that I own, and I love the color. It's a solid ruck too, it's just not quite as refined as the
dedicated Rucker. The bag is linked below, along with the written review. Be sure to like and subscribe for more reviews down the line. That's all for now. Keep rucking.
===== END ITEM youtube:SlW6o3cO2rk =====

