Hey, I'm Joel with Rock Authority and this is the Go Ruck Rucker [music] 5.0. Go Ruck was kind enough to send this over, but they haven't told me what to talk about. For the last week, I've been testing this on the trails [music] up in the Northwoods of Wisconsin. Go Ruck built this Rucker as a training bag. So, the main idea is to keep a weight plate high against [music] your back instead of letting it sag like a normal backpack would. I don't actually have a Rucker [music] 4.0, so I can't do a direct comparison. So, the Rucker 5.0 is an upgrade over the 4.0. And the main differences are that it includes a larger lumbar support. [music] It's 30 mm thick. It looks enormous when you actually see the bag, but once I actually used it, it was very [music] comfortable and I didn't really notice it after a bit. This is the larger 25 [music] L version, but they do make a 20 L version. Just depends on your size and where the lumbar support falls on your back. I'm 6'2", [music] so they sent me the larger one, but everybody's back size is different. The main body is 1,000 [music] denier Cordura. The fabric against your back and underneath the shoulder straps is a smoother 210 denier Cordura. So, it isn't as abrasive [music] against clothing or bare skin. The shoulder straps are thick and they're well padded. The sternum strap [music] can move up or down or come off entirely. There are no load lifters and the padded waist belt [music] is optional. I didn't have one, so I didn't test with it. Inside, you'll see [music] that there's two elevated plate pockets. Got one in the back, one in [music] the front. I did my testing with this 20 lb plate. The shorter pocket fits the Go Ruck standard [music] 10, 20, and 30 lb plates. The longer pocket fits its long 30 and 45 lb plates. You can hold the standard plates too. [music] Together, they're rated up to 75 lbs, although that's a capacity figure, not a suggestion for where to [music] start. And the built-in frame sheet keeps the bag from around it when you tighten the straps here. The bag opens flat. You'll see that these internal [music] pockets now close with magnets. Found this a good place to throw my keys and any [music] assorted little items, snacks, whatever. It's also a place to hang a hydration bladder. You'll see the D-ring, and then there's an opening up here. The zippers are YKK with 550 cord pulls. These are easy to grab, and they don't rattle around when you walk. Outside, you'll get the MOLLE [music] webbing. Got it on the front and on the sides. If you're familiar with MOLLE, there's a whole [music] ecosystem of attachments. There's four padded handles on the top, the sides, [music] and the bottom. The two holes on the bottom are there to drain water. GoRuck describes this bag as highly water resistant. So far, I've been fortunate [music] enough to avoid the rain, so I haven't actually tested that yet. Haven't tried to destroy [music] it, but the stitching around the straps, the handles, and the plate pockets does [music] look substantial. And this bag is protected by the SCARS Lifetime Guarantee. Apparently, some people don't like the big [music] GoRuck branding. They want it more discreet. I don't care. I'm fine to show it. And this has been rock solid. [music] I don't feel it wobbling. It's not shifting weight around. I might have had a bit of a sweaty back, but I'm a bigger [music] guy, so that's going to happen. I didn't have any hot spots or chafing, numbness, or any lower back discomfort. [music] I will say that my traps were a little bit sore, but I think maybe if I were to use the hip belt, then I could alleviate some of the stress up high. Overall, I didn't really notice it while I was walking. This is just kind of [music] an after-the-fact thing. I found there's plenty of space for my extra gear along with the plate. [music] I had a drone case, and I had some camera equipment. If you mainly want an everyday backpack that can occasionally carry [music] some weight, this may be more specialized and more expensive than you need. You give up things like a laptop [music] compartment, an outside bottle pocket, and quick access storage. Another consideration is price. This is obviously a [music] premium bag, and the optional hip belt costs extra. If you want to see the current [music] sizes, colors, and price, the QR code and the link below are affiliate links. I may earn a commission [music] if you buy from the links, but that doesn't change my opinion on the bag. So, if you do want a dedicated [music] bag for carrying plates and doing ruck workouts, I think the Rucker 5.0 does make a lot of sense. >> [music]
